WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.501 --> 00:00:05.267
♪♪

00:00:05.267 --> 00:00:07.501
-You know, you're re-recording.

00:00:07.501 --> 00:00:09.300
This is your second re-record.
-Yes.

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:12.267
I feel like -- I know
that everybody has busy lives,

00:00:12.267 --> 00:00:16.033
so I -- I do feel the need to,
like, explain what I'm doing

00:00:16.033 --> 00:00:18.067
because it's not normal.

00:00:18.067 --> 00:00:19.667
-Yeah.

00:00:19.667 --> 00:00:22.868
-Basically, like, music --

00:00:22.868 --> 00:00:24.434
I've always wanted
to own my own music

00:00:24.434 --> 00:00:26.200
since I started making music,
and if --

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:28.400
You probably don't know this,
but most of your favorite

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:31.133
artists do not own their work.

00:00:31.133 --> 00:00:35.434
The music industry is, uh --
Eh, you know?

00:00:35.434 --> 00:00:36.934
Certain corners of it.

00:00:36.934 --> 00:00:39.234
But I think that there was --

00:00:39.234 --> 00:00:44.100
There was something that
happened years ago where I --

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:45.634
I made it very clear

00:00:45.634 --> 00:00:47.534
that I wanted to be able
to buy my music.

00:00:47.534 --> 00:00:50.133
That opportunity
was not given to me,

00:00:50.133 --> 00:00:52.067
and it was sold
to somebody else.

00:00:52.067 --> 00:00:55.701
And so I just figured, I was the
one who made this music first.

00:00:55.701 --> 00:00:57.567
I can just make it again.

00:00:57.567 --> 00:00:59.334
-Yeah.
-So that's what we're doing.

00:00:59.334 --> 00:01:00.901
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:00.901 --> 00:01:03.734
-I have to say...

00:01:03.734 --> 00:01:05.400
-So that's what we're doing.

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:07.133
So when something says

00:01:07.133 --> 00:01:10.234
"(Taylor's Version)"
next to it,

00:01:10.234 --> 00:01:12.334
that means I own it,
which is exciting.

00:01:12.334 --> 00:01:14.601
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:14.601 --> 00:01:18.167
-It's -- It's quite -- It's
quite a clever loophole, Taylor.

00:01:18.167 --> 00:01:20.067
-Oh, thank you.
-I really tip my cap.

00:01:20.067 --> 00:01:21.534
-Oh, thank you.

00:01:21.534 --> 00:01:24.901
It's -- You know, it's
also interesting to kind of

00:01:24.901 --> 00:01:28.267
go back and relive
this nostalgia with fans

00:01:28.267 --> 00:01:30.934
who are the reason
why I get to do this

00:01:30.934 --> 00:01:32.667
and why I get to, like, sit here

00:01:32.667 --> 00:01:35.701
and, like, have
this lovely chat with you.

00:01:35.701 --> 00:01:38.267
Going back and -- and reliving
these things with the fans.

00:01:38.267 --> 00:01:40.868
And this time around,
I get to really --

00:01:40.868 --> 00:01:42.834
I get to do things
that I know they wish

00:01:42.834 --> 00:01:44.167
I would have done
the first time.

00:01:44.167 --> 00:01:45.534
-That's really good.

00:01:45.534 --> 00:01:47.434
-Because I'm always listening
and I'm always lurking.

00:01:47.434 --> 00:01:48.934
And I'm always listening

00:01:48.934 --> 00:01:51.100
to their opinions
and their theories.

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:52.767
And what --
You know, they'll -- they --

00:01:52.767 --> 00:01:55.467
They will let me know which
songs should have been singles.

00:01:55.467 --> 00:01:57.667
-Yeah.
-They let me know which songs

00:01:57.667 --> 00:02:01.033
did not get videos
and should have gotten videos!

00:02:01.033 --> 00:02:03.033
And so I just like --
You know what?

00:02:03.033 --> 00:02:04.367
Like, I'm listening

00:02:04.367 --> 00:02:07.434
and I'm making the videos
and I'm doing the things.

00:02:07.434 --> 00:02:09.167
[ Cheers and applause ]
-That is really cool.

00:02:09.167 --> 00:02:11.334
You know, I've got three kids.
They're all really young.

00:02:11.334 --> 00:02:13.000
They're under 7,
and I'm trying to

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:15.300
impress my music taste on them.

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:17.200
And I'm wondering,
your kids are older.

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:20.067
Do they like
Bruce Springsteen music?

00:02:20.067 --> 00:02:21.667
-Um...

00:02:21.667 --> 00:02:24.167
At first, they didn't know
any Bruce Springsteen music

00:02:24.167 --> 00:02:26.534
for a very, very, very
long time.

00:02:26.534 --> 00:02:28.534
-How do you keep it from them?

00:02:28.534 --> 00:02:31.000
-Strangers would come up to us

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:33.067
and I used to always have
this thing I'd tell them.

00:02:33.067 --> 00:02:38.234
I'd say, "Look, my job?
I'm Barney for adults, alright?

00:02:38.234 --> 00:02:43.267
And that's why people
keep coming up to us."

00:02:43.267 --> 00:02:45.868
And then eventually,
one of my children came home

00:02:45.868 --> 00:02:48.000
from kindergarten and said,

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:53.868
"Dad, what is
'Tenth Avenue Freeze-Out'?"

00:02:53.868 --> 00:02:57.767
And I said, "Well, it's
a song that Dad wrote."

00:02:57.767 --> 00:02:59.167
"Play it for me."

00:02:59.167 --> 00:03:01.434
I got my guitar,
and I started --

00:03:01.434 --> 00:03:02.567
♪ Tenth Avenue... ♪

00:03:02.567 --> 00:03:04.167
"Play it for real."

00:03:04.167 --> 00:03:05.868
[ Laughter ]

00:03:05.868 --> 00:03:07.334
Okay. [ Chuckles ]

00:03:07.334 --> 00:03:11.200
And I played him the song
and he got it.

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:14.968
And then totally ignored
that part of my life for

00:03:14.968 --> 00:03:17.200
most of the rest of my life,
to this day.

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:20.033
You know, they get
a kick out of it sometimes.

00:03:20.033 --> 00:03:24.234
But for the most part...

00:03:24.234 --> 00:03:27.501
There was --
I always say, no child

00:03:27.501 --> 00:03:31.601
wants to see 50,000 people
cheer their parents.

00:03:31.601 --> 00:03:33.400
[ Laughter ]

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:38.968
You may want to see
50,000 people boo your parents.

00:03:38.968 --> 00:03:40.567
That would be fun.

00:03:40.567 --> 00:03:42.501
That would be interesting.

00:03:42.501 --> 00:03:45.567
But who wants to see 50,000
people cheer their parents?

00:03:45.567 --> 00:03:47.234
No kid does. No kid.

00:03:47.234 --> 00:03:50.968
So we have a very
peaceful coexistence

00:03:50.968 --> 00:03:53.033
where they pretend
it never happened.

00:03:53.033 --> 00:03:55.100
[ Laughter ]

00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:59.267
And, you know,
they picked their own heroes.

00:03:59.267 --> 00:04:01.067
They don't need me.

00:04:01.067 --> 00:04:02.367
-I have a clip
that I want to ask you about.

00:04:02.367 --> 00:04:03.901
-Yes.
-This is from the BET Awards.

00:04:03.901 --> 00:04:06.367
And this -- I'm very impressed
with you here

00:04:06.367 --> 00:04:07.667
because -- let's be honest --

00:04:07.667 --> 00:04:09.434
something goes wrong here

00:04:09.434 --> 00:04:11.567
and you have an ability,
to recover that I'm very --

00:04:11.567 --> 00:04:13.067
Should we show the clip first?

00:04:13.067 --> 00:04:14.300
Yeah, let's just show the clip,
and then we'll talk about it.

00:04:14.300 --> 00:04:15.334
-Are you talking
about the BET --

00:04:15.334 --> 00:04:16.501
-Yeah.
-Are you gonna do that

00:04:16.501 --> 00:04:17.534
to a brother?

00:04:17.534 --> 00:04:18.701
-I'm gonna do it to you
right now.

00:04:18.701 --> 00:04:19.834
-♪ Being a broke... ♪
-Yeah!

00:04:19.834 --> 00:04:21.133
♪ Drinking
more liquor ♪

00:04:21.133 --> 00:04:22.400
♪ Drivin'
a broke Vigor ♪

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:23.467
♪ I'm with
Mo' sippers ♪

00:04:23.467 --> 00:04:24.534
♪ Watched
by gold... ♪

00:04:24.534 --> 00:04:26.334
-[ Laughs ]
You fully --

00:04:26.334 --> 00:04:29.000
So, what happened?
What happened?

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:31.067
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:34.133 --> 00:04:37.467
[ Cheers and applause continue ]

00:04:39.167 --> 00:04:41.734
-Hey. You know what
I tell people all the time?

00:04:41.734 --> 00:04:44.400
♪ I'm only human ♪

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:45.868
[ Laughter ]
-Right? That's good.

00:04:45.868 --> 00:04:48.634
-So, what happened was --

00:04:48.634 --> 00:04:50.167
[ Laughter ]

00:04:50.167 --> 00:04:51.367
But I'm performing
in front of, like --

00:04:51.367 --> 00:04:53.501
-This stuff's great, man,
I tell you.

00:04:53.501 --> 00:04:56.100
-You love it, right?
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:04:56.100 --> 00:04:59.033
Cîroc Apple --
coming to a place near you.

00:04:59.033 --> 00:05:00.701
-Yeah.
-Very soon.

00:05:00.701 --> 00:05:02.200
But check this out.

00:05:02.200 --> 00:05:04.200
I'm performing
in front of, like,

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:07.667
30 million people live on TV.

00:05:07.667 --> 00:05:10.667
I pride myself on being one of
the coolest guys in the world.

00:05:10.667 --> 00:05:12.133
I don't make mistakes like this.

00:05:12.133 --> 00:05:13.467
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
-Okay?

00:05:13.467 --> 00:05:15.501
-So I walk over the trap door.

00:05:15.501 --> 00:05:18.234
Lil' Kim is about to come up.
I get a little excited.

00:05:18.234 --> 00:05:21.200
Kendrick Lamar is in the front.
I'm competitive. I run up.

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:25.000
I'm like, "Kendrick, you see me.
You see me rocking."

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:26.501
-Uh-huh. [ Laughs ]

00:05:26.501 --> 00:05:29.434
-But I forget about
the trap door behind me

00:05:29.434 --> 00:05:30.968
that was closed when

00:05:30.968 --> 00:05:32.634
I was going out to the front.
-Right.

00:05:32.634 --> 00:05:34.834
-And so, then, when
I turned around, it was open.

00:05:34.834 --> 00:05:36.567
-Right.
-Okay?

00:05:36.567 --> 00:05:39.167
But let me tell you this --
there's a part you didn't show.

00:05:39.167 --> 00:05:40.667
I'm a superhero.
-Okay.

00:05:40.667 --> 00:05:43.000
-Okay? I go by the name
of Blackman.

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:44.567
-You just told me
you're only human.

00:05:44.567 --> 00:05:46.434
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:05:46.434 --> 00:05:47.667
-Sometimes.
-Okay. Gotcha.

00:05:47.667 --> 00:05:48.667
-Okay.
-So you're Blackman.

00:05:48.667 --> 00:05:50.000
-So I'm Blackman.

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:51.567
I'm falling through the hole.

00:05:51.567 --> 00:05:55.033
It feels like it's three hours.
It's only like 2.2 seconds.

00:05:55.033 --> 00:05:57.000
-Sure.
-My superpower is I go --

00:05:57.000 --> 00:05:59.901
I put my arms out to the side.
I push myself up.

00:05:59.901 --> 00:06:02.434
I throw myself up in the air.
I land on beat.

00:06:02.434 --> 00:06:04.133
I have this embarrassing
look on my face,

00:06:04.133 --> 00:06:05.767
but I'm not facing the camera.

00:06:05.767 --> 00:06:09.300
And I say to myself, "You better
get that look off your face,"

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:11.667
and turn around, and I'm like...

00:06:11.667 --> 00:06:13.400
[ Laughter ]

00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:17.033
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:17.033 --> 00:06:19.767
-Well, cheers to that,
because I will say...

00:06:19.767 --> 00:06:21.534
every man falls down,
but it's about how

00:06:21.534 --> 00:06:23.200
you pick yourself back up,
and that was...

00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:24.834
-If you fall through a hole...
-Yeah.

00:06:24.834 --> 00:06:26.400
-...get up
-Get back up.

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:27.968
-Do you think aliens are real?

00:06:27.968 --> 00:06:29.667
-I know that they're real.

00:06:29.667 --> 00:06:30.934
-Gotcha.
This isn't a "think" thing.

00:06:30.934 --> 00:06:32.634
-No, this isn't a thing --
How --

00:06:32.634 --> 00:06:36.334
How self-centered would we be
as humans to believe

00:06:36.334 --> 00:06:41.000
that we are the only
living things in the universe?

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:42.834
-Um, well,
call me self-centered,

00:06:42.834 --> 00:06:44.300
'cause I don't believe
in aliens.

00:06:44.300 --> 00:06:46.033
[ Laughter ]
-Well...

00:06:46.033 --> 00:06:48.067
[ Laughter ]

00:06:48.067 --> 00:06:50.767
I'm also just, like, a huge fan
of conspiracy theories.

00:06:50.767 --> 00:06:52.000
-You love conspiracy theories.
-Yes.

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:54.133
-You -- This is,
I think, my favorite

00:06:54.133 --> 00:06:57.200
'cause I heard that you are --
you're all-in on mermaids.

00:06:57.200 --> 00:06:59.267
-Okay, I can explain.
-Great.

00:06:59.267 --> 00:07:00.767
-Okay.
-I would like you to.

00:07:00.767 --> 00:07:02.834
-So, you know Atlantis --

00:07:02.834 --> 00:07:04.300
how there was
that underground sea?

00:07:04.300 --> 00:07:05.634
-Yeah.
-Okay. Or, no.

00:07:05.634 --> 00:07:07.300
-An underground city.
-You know what I mean.

00:07:07.300 --> 00:07:08.567
-Sorry. Underwater city.
-Yes.

00:07:08.567 --> 00:07:09.801
-Yeah.
-So I believe --

00:07:09.801 --> 00:07:12.467
-We're being very convincing.
-I know. I know.

00:07:12.467 --> 00:07:15.000
I'm really convincing.
-We're off to a great start.

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:16.868
[ Both laughing ]
-Yeah, right?

00:07:16.868 --> 00:07:21.801
No, I believe that there could
possibly be mermaids,

00:07:21.801 --> 00:07:24.334
which is a --
actually an alien species

00:07:24.334 --> 00:07:26.834
that lives in parts
of the Indian Ocean,

00:07:26.834 --> 00:07:29.267
which we have never explored
before as human beings.

00:07:29.267 --> 00:07:30.868
-Okay.
-And Columbus --

00:07:30.868 --> 00:07:33.300
Christopher Columbus
had actually seen three mermaids

00:07:33.300 --> 00:07:35.968
on his way to America.

00:07:35.968 --> 00:07:37.834
-Yeah. Alright.
So...

00:07:37.834 --> 00:07:39.267
-I just think it's --
it's possible.

00:07:39.267 --> 00:07:41.767
And there's this, like, really
extremely convincing documentary

00:07:41.767 --> 00:07:43.367
that came out.
-Gotcha.

00:07:43.367 --> 00:07:44.801
"The Little Mermaid"?
-Mnh.

00:07:44.801 --> 00:07:46.634
[ Laughter ]

00:07:46.634 --> 00:07:47.767
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:47.767 --> 00:07:50.000
[ Both laugh ]

00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:53.501
-No! It was on, like,
National Geogra--

00:07:53.501 --> 00:07:55.367
No, Discovery Channel.
-Gotcha.

00:07:55.367 --> 00:07:59.234
-And then, like, the next day,
they had to say it was fake,

00:07:59.234 --> 00:08:00.734
and then it went missing.

00:08:00.734 --> 00:08:01.901
-Oh, 'cause they were because
the mermaid lobby was like,

00:08:01.901 --> 00:08:03.801
"Hey, you be quiet."
-Yes!

00:08:03.801 --> 00:08:05.234
Hey, you know what?

00:08:05.234 --> 00:08:07.734
-Look, you can -- this
is the most fun I've had...

00:08:07.734 --> 00:08:09.100
-You brought it up.
-...during an interview.

00:08:09.100 --> 00:08:10.701
Yeah, I did bring it up.
That's true.

00:08:10.701 --> 00:08:11.934
Alright, look,
nothing would make me happier

00:08:11.934 --> 00:08:13.367
than mermaids being real.

00:08:13.367 --> 00:08:15.834
I'd rather live in your world
than mine, where I'm a cynic

00:08:15.834 --> 00:08:17.267
and I just wake up
every morning and be like,

00:08:17.267 --> 00:08:18.701
"Well, I'm not going to see
a mermaid."

00:08:18.701 --> 00:08:19.901
[ Laughter ]

00:08:19.901 --> 00:08:22.868
-It must be really sad, then,
for you to not...

00:08:22.868 --> 00:08:24.267
-Yeah.
-...believe that mermaids exist.

00:08:24.267 --> 00:08:25.868
-Do you think they look
like the mermaids of books?

00:08:25.868 --> 00:08:26.934
-No.
-Okay.

00:08:26.934 --> 00:08:29.501
-No. I mean, they're aliens.

00:08:29.501 --> 00:08:31.133
[ Laughter ]

00:08:31.133 --> 00:08:32.367
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:08:32.367 --> 00:08:33.601
-You mentioned your family.

00:08:33.601 --> 00:08:35.334
You have a very loving,
supportive family.

00:08:35.334 --> 00:08:37.667
You also might have
the world's best godmother.

00:08:37.667 --> 00:08:39.000
-Yeah.
-Dolly Parton.

00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:40.067
-I'm pretty lucky.
-Yeah.

00:08:40.067 --> 00:08:42.067
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:08:42.067 --> 00:08:44.400
-Yeah. I'm pretty lucky.
-You guys did a Super Bowl

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:45.734
commercial together.
-Yes.

00:08:45.734 --> 00:08:47.667
-You've done album work
together.

00:08:47.667 --> 00:08:49.400
-Which, it was amazing because,
you know, she was doing

00:08:49.400 --> 00:08:52.133
it for the phones, yet every
time I coordinate and kind of

00:08:52.133 --> 00:08:54.400
communicate with Dolly,
it's still through fax.

00:08:54.400 --> 00:08:55.400
-You have to --
-So I don't know

00:08:55.400 --> 00:08:57.067
what she's talking about.

00:08:57.067 --> 00:08:58.667
She's rarely on the phone.

00:08:58.667 --> 00:09:00.334
Next time we have to do it for
the fax machine.

00:09:00.334 --> 00:09:01.834
-So if you see her
in a commercial talking about

00:09:01.834 --> 00:09:03.734
how great phones are, just know
that's some false advertising?

00:09:03.734 --> 00:09:07.834
-We do use the phone, but she
does a fax, and then someone

00:09:07.834 --> 00:09:10.968
scans the fax, and then they put
it into a text message,

00:09:10.968 --> 00:09:12.300
and then that gets sent to me.

00:09:12.300 --> 00:09:13.901
-No.
-Yeah.

00:09:13.901 --> 00:09:15.634
And it's always signed.

00:09:15.634 --> 00:09:17.767
So now I've kind of started
my own version where I go

00:09:17.767 --> 00:09:21.834
into the notes, and I've started
to try to make my own letters,

00:09:21.834 --> 00:09:25.467
because there's something so
amazing just about a connection.

00:09:25.467 --> 00:09:27.901
It's just not casual, you know?

00:09:27.901 --> 00:09:29.801
You know that she took the time.
-Yes.

00:09:29.801 --> 00:09:32.267
-To get out, I guess
her typewriter?

00:09:32.267 --> 00:09:37.434
She also made me a demo one time
where she talks about being

00:09:37.434 --> 00:09:40.767
super high-tech, but it was all
through a cassette,

00:09:40.767 --> 00:09:43.667
which then she voice memo'd
onto a flip phone,

00:09:43.667 --> 00:09:45.734
which someone then
put onto the iPhone,

00:09:45.734 --> 00:09:47.067
which then got sent to me.

00:09:47.067 --> 00:09:49.667
-I like that she recorded it
on a flip phone,

00:09:49.667 --> 00:09:51.367
then she FedEx'd you
the flip phone?

00:09:51.367 --> 00:09:53.534
-Yes. She has a phone.

00:09:53.534 --> 00:09:55.200
She just doesn't use it,
I don't think.

00:09:55.200 --> 00:09:58.400
I think someone uses it for her
